The newest editions (3 and 3.5) are called D&D again. Decide how much you
want to spend on books, and guestimate how long you'll be interested in
it. Second edition has lots of materials, but is no longer 'supported'.
You may be able to get it secondhand for cheaper (or more expenisive, if
people decide it has become collectors items). Third edition (I think 3.5
is a bugfix, so those would proably be preforable over 3 when available)
has much different rules, but which are supposed to be more expandable to
different sorts of games.
I think Wizards has a downloadable _basic_ rules to get started. And don't
be afraid to implement house rules (eg: no psionics).

You can do a lot of roleplaying, even if the game is dice-based. It all
depends on your DM and players.

